    {
      "id": "b2",
      "function": "problem",
      "scene_type": "rv_chart",
      "spoken": "In a p-V diagram, pressure in the cylinder goes up the side and volume goes along the bottom. Each stroke draws a line. During compression the volume shrinks and the pressure rises. On the far left is top dead centre with the smallest volume, on the far right bottom dead centre.",
      "data": {
        "title": "Pressure over volume",
        "kind": "pv",
        "series_at": [
          "w:compression"
        ],
        "series_only": [
          0
        ],
        "legend": true,
        "fade_in": false,
        "fade_out": false
      },
      "overlay": {
        "marker": "fakt",
        "marker_at": "w:pressure",
        "kicker": "2 · THE AXES",
        "transition": {
          "type": "blende",
          "seconds": 0.7
        }
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b3",
      "function": "explanation",
      "scene_type": "rv_cutaway",
      "spoken": "Then combustion. In NASA's ideal Otto cycle it happens very fast, at constant volume: the pressure jumps straight up. After that the gas expands and pushes the piston down. The NASA model assumes no friction and no heat loss, an ideal case.",
      "data": {
        "title": "Same space, more pressure",
        "labels": [
          {
            "key": "plug",
            "side": "l",
            "y": 640
          },
          "chamber"
        ],
        "labels_at": [
          "w:combustion",
          "w:volume"
        ],
        "notes": [
          {
            "text": "pressure jumps",
            "at": "w:jumps",
            "side": "l",
            "y": 880,
            "color": "fire",
            "to": [
              540,
              560
            ]
          }
        ],
        "theta": [
          [
            0,
            300
          ],
          [
            1,
            500
          ]
        ],
        "fade_in": false,
        "fade_out": false
      },
      "overlay": {
        "marker": "fakt",
        "marker_at": "w:Otto",
        "kicker": "3 · COMBUSTION",
        "transition": {
          "type": "blende",
          "seconds": 0.7
        }
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b4",
      "function": "demonstration",
      "scene_type": "rv_chart",
      "spoken": "Together they form a closed loop. The enclosed area is the work of one cycle, as NASA puts it. Multiply by the cycles per second and you get power. A bigger loop means more work per cycle.",
      "data": {
        "title": "Area = work",
        "kind": "pv",
        "series_at": [
          0.0,
          0.08,
          0.16,
          0.24
        ],
        "area_at": "w:area",
        "draw_seconds": 0.8,
        "fade_in": false,
        "fade_out": false
      },
      "overlay": {
        "marker": "fakt",
        "marker_at": "w:NASA",
        "kicker": "4 · THE AREA",
        "transition": {
          "type": "blende",
          "seconds": 0.7
        }
